	url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/092054899390035P},
	doi = {10.1016/0920-5489(93)90035-P},
	abstract = {Language bindings have hitherto mostly been thought of as specifying how a particular language accesses external functionality which is defined in a language-independent way. These bindings have so far usually been provided by those defining the functionality. This paper identifies three further kinds of binding and the problems these present. It concludes that, for two if not all three of these, the bindings need to be done within the language community, in particular by implementors, but that there may be reluctance to undertake it - which would mean that language users would be deprived of the benefits of the language-independent specification.},
